Situation:
The division is advancing along multiple axes into contested terrain. Sustainment is being pushed from a network of rear and intermediate depots (e.g., Logistics Base Sentinel, Supply Depot Bravo, Depot Hammer) to dispersed forward units (e.g., Vanguard Infantry Battalion, Saber Armored Battalion, Guardian Fires Battalion, Recon Squadron Echo). Lines of communication include a valley main supply route exposed to enemy fires and observation, an urban corridor prone to ambush and choke points, a mountain pass with weather and mobility restrictions, and a desert track with extended standoff but longer distances. Each movement corridor requires engineer route clearance, security escort, and overwatch to mitigate IEDs, indirect fire, and ambush threats. Stocks at depots vary by day, and unit demands fluctuate with tempo and contact. The planning horizon is daily and time-sensitive.

Mission:
Deliver the required daily resupply to all supported units while minimizing total operational cost. Costs include transportation by route, the penalty for any accepted shortfall, the cost of deploying vehicles, and a fixed security risk cost incurred each day a route is activated.

Execution/Concept:
- We will determine, day by day, which depot supplies each unit, which ground routes to activate, and how many vehicles to assign per active route. Flows are continuous; convoy counts are discrete; route activation is a binary decision.
- Depot issues cannot exceed available daily stocks. Supported unit demands must be met; limited shortfalls are allowed only as a last resort and will incur penalties until recovered.
- Vehicle capacity limits apply at the route level; the shipped volume must fit within assigned convoy capacity. A finite fleet is shared across all routes each day.
- Routes can only carry vehicles if activated. Critically, activating a route on any day incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing security overhead (route clearance, escorts, overwatch, QRF), regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route that day.
- Transportation costs vary by depot–unit pairing and day, reflecting distance, road condition, and exposure. Vehicle deployment has its own cost per vehicle-day. Shortage penalties apply per unit and day of unmet demand.
- Preference is to consolidate loads on fewer activated corridors when risk overhead is high, while maintaining redundancy for survivability. When stocks or threats change, flows will be reallocated among depots and routes to preserve flexibility and minimize total cost.
- Commanders retain authority to accept limited shortfalls where the combined risk and movement costs exceed the penalty threshold, but essential combat loads for maneuver and fires will be prioritized to maintain momentum.

